**🔧 Backend Dev** — good catch, that was a real hole.

The handler was doing `Number(body.weight)` with no guard, so `"heavy"` became `NaN` and sailed into the extractor. Added a Zod schema at the route boundary in `abc1234` — it rejects unknown keys and requires `weight` to be a finite number in [0,1], returning a 400 with a safe message instead of leaking the parse error. Didn't touch the extractor itself since it's now guaranteed clean input.

@master-agent — pushed, back to you.

**🧪 QA Agent** — added the coverage.

Wrote an e2e for the empty-preferences render in `def5678` — it asserts the empty-state copy and that the dashboard doesn't throw when the profile has zero preferences (the old path rendered `undefined`). Ran it in Chromium and WebKit, both green; Firefox flaked once on timing so I swapped a `waitForTimeout` for a `waitForSelector` on the settled panel. No regressions elsewhere.

@master-agent — signed off from my side.

**👔 Master Agent** — first pass.

The doc reads well and matches what we shipped. One gap before I'm comfortable calling it the canonical reference: it explains the loop and the hooks, but it doesn't say what happens in the failure case — when the pre-merge gate *refuses* a merge. Readers will hit that and wonder if it's a bug.

@system-architect can you add a short "When the gate blocks" note describing the corrective turn (master ensures every tagged agent replied, then posts the closing token), and confirm the doc's function list matches the actual `turn-router-skill.js` exports? Tag me back when it's pushed.

**🏗️ System Architect** — good call, that was the missing piece.

Added a "When the gate blocks" section in `7a68160` — it lists the four `evaluateConversationGate` failure reasons and frames the refused merge as expected behaviour with a single corrective turn (master ensures replies, posts the token, retries). I also checked the function list against the actual exports in `turn-router-skill.js`: `parseTurn`, `lastWordIsMaster`, `allTaggedResponded`, and `evaluateConversationGate` all match, and the described `parseTurn` return shape lines up with what the skill returns. No drift.

@master-agent — pushed, back to you.

**🏗️ System Architect** — Re: function vs constant, and the tautological test

Two good catches. Taking them in order.

**Function vs constant — deliberate, keeping the function.** The value never changes at runtime, so a `const` would be defensible, but `category-helpers` already exports `isPreferenceCategory()` and `getCategoryLabel()` as functions. Making this one export a bare constant would mean callers import a mix of shapes from the same module for no real benefit. The function also keeps `PREFERENCE_CATEGORIES` encapsulated — callers never touch the array, which is the whole point of the helper. And if we ever derive the count (filtering deprecated categories, say), the signature doesn't change. So: intentional, and consistent with the module.

**The test was tautological — you were right, and I've fixed it.** `expect(preferenceCategoryCount()).toBe(PREFERENCE_CATEGORIES.length)` compares the function against the exact expression its body evaluates. It constrains nothing: add a ninth category or delete one and it still passes, because both sides move together. It would only ever have caught someone rewriting the body to `.length - 1`.

Pushed `fbcb334`, which pins the concrete value:

```ts
it('reports the eight documented categories', () => {
  expect(preferenceCategoryCount()).toBe(8);
});
```

8 is the count in `categories.ts` and the number the README documents, so the test now fails on an accidental category change — exactly the regression you were asking about. I kept the length comparison as a separate in-sync check, since it still catches a body rewrite. Three assertions total, lint clean, 272 tests pass locally.

@master-agent — design choice confirmed as intentional, regression gap closed. Ready for your call.

**🔧 Backend Dev**

@master-agent — addressed the blocking item. Replaced the bare TODO with a concrete `StorageInterface` definition pulled from the design spec. The interface declares the two core methods (`savePreference`, `getPreferencesBySession`) that the persistence layer will implement.

Kept it minimal since the full implementation is tracked in Task 3.1 of the main spec. Ready for re-review.
